<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.11.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00237</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.11.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>817841be-2063-447b-8e44-eb767c04b525</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>For numbers 1–2, use the graph below:</para>
<figure><media alt="This figure a shows a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (negative 6, negative 8), (negative 4, negative 6), (negative 2, negative 4), (0, negative 2), (2, 0), (4, 2), (6, 4), (8, 6)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b43d81b1ae710bc8b78c0278758a16ed71d489d9"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the graph?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((0,2)\)</item>
  <item>\((2,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((0,-2)\)</item>
  <item>\((-2,0)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\( (2,0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((0,2)\)</item>
  <item>\((2,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((0,-2)\)</item>
  <item>\((-2,0)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \((0,-2)\)</para>
<para>For numbers 3–4, use the graph below:</para>
<figure><media alt="This figure a shows a straight line graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y axes run from negative 10 to 10. The line goes through the points (negative 6, 6), (negative 3, 4), (0, 2), (3, 0), (6, negative 2), and (9, negative 4)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7825f9d07108bf3c79a1b240974ac6f172bdfca7"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the graph?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((0,2)\)</item>
  <item>\((2,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((0,3)\)</item>
  <item>\((3,0)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> (3,0)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>What are the coordinates of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((0,2)\)</item>
  <item>\((2,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((0,3)\)</item>
  <item>\((3,0)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> (0,2)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>What are the coordinates of the \(x\)-intercept of the equation \(3x + y = 12\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((0,12)\)</item>
  <item>\((12,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((4,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((0,4)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> (4,0)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the \(y\)-intercept of the equation \(3x + y = 12\)?</item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\((0,12\))</item>
  <item>\((12,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((4,0)\)</item>
  <item>\((0,4)\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> (0,12)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the slope of the line \(3x- 2y=6\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(\frac32\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the \(y\)-intercept of \(y = 6x + 2\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> 2. In \(y = mx + b\), the \(b\) is the \(y\)-intercept. In this equation, \(b = 2\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the \(y\)-intercept of \(10x + 5y = 30\)?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> 6</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which graph represents the equation \( 12=3x+4y \)? Explain how you know.</item>
</list>
<list type="enumerated">
  <item><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a6168a6019660ea9b265ba1bac0348b9b39141e2"/></media></figure></item>
  <item><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3505fd868a59a904a12adbf1667c928455c2ebcd"/></media></figure></item>
  <item><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2a2eb8b183db689ad120aa6b36d0452cbbd0dfcf"/></media></figure></item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>a<figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a6168a6019660ea9b265ba1bac0348b9b39141e2"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>When \(x = 0\), \(12 = 3(0) + 4y\) gives \(y = 3\). The \(y\)-intercept is at (0, 3). When \(y = 0\), \(12 = 3x + 4(0)\) gives \(x = 4\). The \(x\)-intercept is (4, 0). Graph \(a\) has these intercepts.</para>
</section>
</content>
</document>
